  "meta": {
    "title": "The Invisible Highways Planes Use Over the Atlantic ✈️🌊🤯",
    "description": "Planes crossing the North Atlantic ✈️ don't fly random routes. They follow the North Atlantic Tracks, invisible highways that are re-drawn twice a day to follow the jet stream 🌬️ The jet stream is a river of wind about 30,000 ft up, blowing west to east, sometimes up to 400 km/h! Flights to Europe ride it as a tailwind, flights to America avoid it. About 500,000 flights used the system in 2018 🤯",
    "pinnedComment": [
      "The sky has highways that move every day ✈️🤯",
      "Have you noticed flights to Europe are faster? 👇",
      "Which aviation secret should we explain next? 🗺️"
    ],
